      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack discipline in following them, but because they follow them too faithfully. The roadmap, meant to be an instrument of strategy, becomes an instrument of obligation — and once that inversion happens, failure is nearly guaranteed.\n\n**They promise certainty the future cannot supply.**\nA typical roadmap presents next quarter and next year with identical confidence: neat bars on a timeline, features assigned to dates. But product development is a search problem. What a team will build in nine months depends on what it learns in the next three, and that learning hasn't happened yet. False precision doesn't reduce risk; it hides it. When reality intervenes, the team faces a choice between honesty and credibility, and credibility usually wins — so the roadmap is quietly defended long after it stopped being true.\n\n**They measure output instead of outcome.**\nShipping fourteen of fifteen planned features looks like success and is reported as such. But no customer ever wanted a feature; they wanted a problem solved. A roadmap organized around deliverables gives teams no way to notice that a shipped feature failed, and no incentive to look. Success becomes adherence, and adherence is the one metric that can be perfect while the business declines.\n\n**They are political documents.**\nRoadmaps are frequently assembled by aggregating requests — sales needs this to close a deal, the executive sponsor wants that, support has a list. The result is a negotiated settlement rather than a strategy. Strategy requires saying no, and a roadmap built to make everyone feel heard has said no to nothing. Such roadmaps are always overcommitted, because their real function is reassurance, not planning.\n\n**They ignore the actual cost of running a product.**\nPlans routinely allocate one hundred percent of capacity to new work, leaving nothing for defects, migrations, security, infrastructure decay, or the discovery work needed to make the next decision well. The unplanned work doesn't disappear; it consumes the plan from within, and every roadmap slips for reasons that were entirely foreseeable.\n\n**They lack a mechanism for change.**\nA plan without a revision process is a wish. Most organizations have elaborate rituals for creating roadmaps and none for revisiting them. Nobody is authorized to remove an item, so items accumulate. New information arrives and finds no door to enter through.\n\nThe alternative is not to abandon planning. Teams need shared direction, and stakeholders legitimately need to coordinate. But a roadmap works better when it is organized around problems rather than solutions, when confidence is expressed honestly — committed, next, later — and when it is reviewed on a known cadence with explicit permission to change course. Above all, it works when the surrounding organization judges teams by the outcomes they produce rather than the promises they keep.\n\nRoadmaps fail when they are asked to be contracts. They succeed when they remain arguments: this is what we believe, this is why, and this is what would change our minds.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, set expectations, and guide execution toward valuable outcomes. Yet most fail—not because planning is inherently flawed, but because they are treated as rigid contracts rather than living hypotheses. The result is missed deadlines, wasted effort, eroded trust, and products that miss the market.\n\nA core reason is the absence of a clear strategy. Many roadmaps are feature lists disconnected from business goals or customer problems. Teams commit to shipping “the dashboard,” “the mobile app,” or “AI features” without defining success metrics or the underlying jobs-to-be-done. When priorities shift or data contradicts assumptions, the plan has no north star, so work continues on items that no longer matter.\n\nOvercommitment compounds the problem. Stakeholders demand dates and scope; product managers, seeking alignment, pack the timeline with more than capacity allows. Engineering realities—technical debt, unknowns, dependencies—are underestimated. The roadmap becomes a promise rather than a forecast, guaranteeing disappointment when delivery slips.\n\nPoor communication and lack of shared ownership accelerate failure. A roadmap created in isolation by product or leadership is rarely understood or believed by engineering, design, sales, or customers. Without regular, transparent updates and genuine input, it is ignored or gamed. Cross-functional dependencies surface too late, and political pressure to “hit the date” overrides learning.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps ignore change. Markets, competitors, user behavior, and technology evolve. Treating the document as static—rather than a set of bets that must be validated, killed, or adjusted—guarantees irrelevance. Teams that refuse to re-prioritize or cut scope when evidence arrives waste quarters on the original plan.\n\nSuccessful roadmaps invert these patterns: they start from outcomes, remain visibly tentative, involve the people who will execute them, and are revisited frequently with data. The ones that fail do the opposite. They confuse activity with progress and commitment with certainty. In a world of uncertainty, that is a recipe for waste.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
